            title: 'Tartli',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Tartli (FelsÅ‘s) is a two-player trick-and-draw game from Hungary, played with a 32-card German-suited deck (Acorns, Leaves, Hearts, Gourds). Part of the Jass family; the Over-Knave (FelsÅ‘) is the highest trump (unlike most Jass where Under-Knave is highest). Players declare combinations at start and during playâ€”sets of equal cards and sequences in suit. Popular in Hungary around the turn of the 19thâ€“20th century (e.g. SÃ¡ndor BrÃ³dy); declined by mid-1920s. Austrian version called Tartl.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Europe\nTime Period: Early 1900s\nHistorical Development: The game evolved from traditional card games in the region\nCultural Significance: Popular social and family game',
